        </p>
        <p class="eyebrow">Essay</p>
        <h1>Does reverse engineering need a bigger model?</h1>
        <p class="page-meta">By N0zoM1z0 · 13 October 2026</p>
        <div class="blog-takeaway">
          <p class="eyebrow">Takeaway</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong
                >Model capability changes the hypotheses an agent can
                find.</strong
              >
              Later sessions have solved problems that earlier work left
              unresolved.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong
                >A familiar compiler gives specialization something concrete to
                learn.</strong
              >
              Some exact matches depend on narrow, reusable source patterns.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Give repository knowledge a fair comparison.</strong>
              A smaller model with good retrieval may already know enough to
              make progress.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong
                >Train the next useful experiment, and test its oracle.</strong
              >
              The value of feedback depends on what the checker actually
              verifies.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong
                >Measure accepted reconstruction against time and cost.</strong
              >
              That is the result I want from the whole agent.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="article-layout">
        <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
          <p>In this article</p>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#model-capability">What better models changed</a></li>
            <li><a href="#specialization">Why specialize?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#repository-knowledge">Where experience lives</a></li>
            <li><a href="#learning-search">Learning the next experiment</a></li>
            <li><a href="#re-and-rl">RE and reinforcement learning</a></li>
            <li><a href="#oracle">When the oracle is wrong</a></li>
            <li><a href="#comparison">A comparison worth running</a></li>
            <li><a href="#future-agent">What kind of agent?</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
        <article
          class="article"
          aria-label="Models and reverse engineering essay"
        >
          <section class="article-section" id="model-capability">
            <h2>What better models changed</h2>
            <p>
              Since August 2026, I have been exploring how much reverse
              engineering an agent can carry out with access to a program and a
              way to check its work. Most of my experience comes from
              reconstructing games, especially Touhou. As I have changed models,
              the repositories have kept a record of what each session managed
              to recover.
            </p>
            <p>
              Much of that work aims at exact reconstruction: we compile the
              maintained source under a pinned historical toolchain and check
              whether it reproduces the target function. When a later model
              succeeds where earlier work stalled, the source changes show what
              made that progress possible.
            </p>
            <h3>The temporary values had to stay alive together</h3>
            <p>
              Much of a TH09 attack function's logic had already been
              reconstructed, but the compiler still stored temporary results
              differently from the original. The remaining problem was how long
              those results needed to stay alive and when their storage could be
              reused.
            </p>
            <p>
              A
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/de63c0f9121963f3a6b1ab5471c4878625fd3425"
                >GPT-6-Sol checkpoint</a
              >
              recorded this mismatch after testing several ordinary source
              arrangements. When
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/52929ed7c299f1a5f29cc13964e22510145fd616"
                >GPT-6.1-Sol returned to the problem</a
              >, it found a useful hypothesis about how long those temporary
              values needed to stay alive.
            </p>
            <p>
              The function produced temporary results and copied them into
              animation positions. In C++, a temporary result stays alive until
              the expression using it finishes. Grouping the real operations
              into one expression kept those results alive together, so the
              compiler could no longer reuse the same storage for all of them.
            </p>
            <p>
              One result still shared storage with a value used later for
              collision checks. Giving that value its own lifetime resolved the
              remaining overlap. After several targeted experiments,
              <strong
                >a clean rebuild confirmed that the full function
                matched</strong
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              What interests me is how the discovery and the tests worked
              together. The lifetime hypothesis gave the agent a new direction;
              compiler feedback helped it refine that hypothesis into a source
              arrangement that matched.
            </p>
            <h3>A different control-flow graph</h3>
            <p>
              A second TH09 function shows why that new direction can matter.
              Here, the obstacle was how several branches shared their final
              work.
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/f8d12d72aaf02212ee1b585a8f3f86d7ed000269"
                >GPT-6.1-Sol's earlier work</a
              >
              got parts of the function to match, but the branches still joined
              up differently from the original.
            </p>
            <p>
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/da33a646221198ceb7f3df2b96e2ca0e9b2f705f"
                >GPT-6-Astra changed the surrounding control-flow graph</a
              >. The main paths now led to a shared final update and return. In
              that source structure, the compiler combined the branches in the
              same way as the original. The complete function matched, and a
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/5f825b28a862f8e1a6d4aea2f78a42f04c09a1c0"
                >separate rebuild check confirmed it</a
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >Changing the representation opened a more productive search
                space.</strong
              >
              Both cases give me a reason to take general model capability
              seriously: an agent can keep trying local variations for a long
              time before finding a better way to express the surrounding work.
            </p>
            <p>
              At the same time, the later sessions started with earlier source
              and recorded failures. That accumulated work was part of their
              advantage. The history lets us inspect the discoveries and their
              checks; a controlled comparison would help separate the effect of
              the model from the experience it inherited.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is where the question becomes interesting to me. Some of the
              progress came from finding a new representation, while much of the
              remaining work involved testing source forms against a familiar
              compiler. How much needs broad reasoning ability, and how much
              could a model learn through repeated experience with that compiler
              and code?
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="specialization">
            <h2>Why might a specialist do well?</h2>
            <p>
              Touhou gives us a concrete reason to expect that experience to
              help. Much of the game code comes from the same author, ZUN, so
              recurring habits are a reasonable starting assumption. The
              repositories back that up with useful examples: related loops and
              helper arrangements have supplied hypotheses across games.
            </p>
            <p>
              Those source patterns become more specific when we include the
              compiler. Under a particular version and set of options, small
              changes in source can produce repeatable differences in machine
              code. Some of those differences are surprisingly obscure.
            </p>
            <p>
              In TH095, for example,
              <strong
                >even the name used for a local variable could change where
                VC7.1 stored it</strong
              >. Moving the declaration alone did not solve the layout, but an
              earlier background function had revealed a naming pattern that
              affected the compiler's choice. That gave us a more promising
              hypothesis for
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th095/commit/26bb64bdb093a13f73abd0ee8ae7ce199ee4658f"
                >the reconstruction of <code>Supervisor::LoadDat</code></a
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              Reusing that pattern for the file-size variable produced the
              required layout without adding storage. The full function then
              matched.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is the sort of narrow detail a general model may not know
              when it arrives. A specialist trained on this compiler could
              encounter many such cases and learn which source variations are
              worth testing first.
            </p>
            <p>
              The opportunity is especially clear in final matching problems
              where we already understand the behavior. We are then asking which
              ordinary source form this compiler turns into the observed
              instructions. Specialization could help the model propose those
              forms more efficiently, while the target still determines which
              hypotheses survive.
            </p>
            <p>
              The useful patterns would have to retain their context, though.
              TH08 uses an earlier Visual C++ version than TH095 and TH09. TH04
              uses a different DOS-era compiler, and its
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04/commit/6dce0a79d5b931f0e2a08475b90e0c33d2321b98"
                >player reconstruction</a
              >
              needed different hypotheses about source patterns and how the
              compiled code was combined into an executable. Shared authorship
              gives us a prior, but each target tells us whether it applies.
              Distinguishing those contexts is part of what a specialist would
              need to learn.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="repository-knowledge">
            <h2>Where should that experience live?</h2>
            <p>
              Before deciding to teach those patterns through model training, I
              would look at the knowledge we already keep in the repository. In
              the
              <a href="../touhou-reconstruction/">previous essay</a>, I wrote
              about it becoming the project's working memory. That memory
              includes compiler lessons, along with the source and failed
              experiments that made them useful.
            </p>
            <p>
              By retrieving that record, an agent can start with a known result
              and inspect how we reached it. It can also avoid alternatives that
              have already failed in the same context. The starting point
              improves without changing the model's weights.
            </p>
            <p>
              When reconstructing TH09's enemy-spawn function, a
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/1eb843383941d00065622a9e353146c4bb96979e"
                >GPT-6-Luna-max session</a
              >
              drew a hypothesis from TH08's corresponding source family. That
              earlier source suggested how to structure the loop and handle
              failures within it. TH09's own evidence supplied the game-specific
              details, and clean rebuilds confirmed the complete match.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >"The model did not know that compiler quirk" and "the model
                could not use it" are different problems.</strong
              >
              Retrieval may solve the first, but turning that fact into a useful
              candidate still takes work. And if the candidate fails, the model
              must decide what the result means for its next attempt. Knowing
              the relevant pattern is only the beginning of that process.
            </p>
            <p>
              For that reason, I would include a smaller general model with good
              retrieval in any comparison. Giving it the same library of
              experience would help us identify what training adds: better
              candidate generation, better decisions after feedback, or both.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="learning-search">
            <h2>Learning the next useful experiment</h2>
            <p>
              Those decisions after feedback are what I would most like to
              study. In agentic reconstruction, the model proposes an
              explanation and tests it against the program. Even when the
              candidate fails, the result can narrow the remaining possibilities
              and suggest a more useful next experiment.
            </p>
            <p>
              Consider the temporary-results example again. Its final source
              shows the arrangement that worked, but the route to that source
              contains more information. Each of the following experiments
              helped identify what still needed to change.
            </p>
            <div class="evidence-table-wrap">
              <table
                class="evidence-table"
                aria-label="Selected reconstruction experiments"
              >
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Experiment</th>
                    <th scope="col">What its result taught us</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Try a different way to copy the results</td>
                    <td>
                      The copy code changes, but the function still differs.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Use the returned values directly</td>
                    <td>
                      Copying follows the original pattern, but temporary
                      storage still differs.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Keep the temporary results alive together</td>
                    <td>
                      They get separate storage, but one still overlaps a value
                      used later for collision checks.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Keep the collision-check value separate</td>
                    <td>A clean rebuild confirms the complete match.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="caption">
              Selected experiments from the attack-function reconstruction
              above, grouped by what they taught us.
            </p>
            <p>
              A training record that keeps this sequence can teach more than the
              final source alone. It connects each proposed change to an
              observed result and the next decision. Keeping ineffective
              variations matters too: they tell a later agent which hypotheses
              have already been tested in that context.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >I would want the model to learn how to use feedback.</strong
              >
              A neutral result is a reason to reconsider the variation; a
              changed mismatch may point to another cause. When local edits stop
              being informative, the agent should be able to revisit the source
              representation or inspect the evidence again.
            </p>
            <p>
              Learning to make those choices requires autonomy. Sometimes the
              useful next step is inspecting another caller rather than
              compiling another edit. A rigid sequence of required rewrites
              would remove that choice, along with part of the behavior we want
              the model to learn.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="re-and-rl">
            <h2>Why RE is interesting for reinforcement learning</h2>
            <p>
              Bounded reconstruction gives us a practical environment for
              studying these choices. Once the target and compiler are pinned,
              many source experiments produce quick, repeatable feedback. The
              agent can compile a candidate, inspect the mismatch and use that
              observation to choose its next step.
            </p>
            <p>
              Reinforcement learning could train that decision-making process
              through the feedback it receives. A candidate accepted by a
              defined check gives us a concrete outcome, while the sequence of
              attempts lets us observe how much work it took to reach it.
            </p>
            <p>
              There is already research supporting this direction.
              <a href="https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.203/"
                >LLM4Decompile</a
              >
              studies models specialized for decompilation.
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.16162v1">AutoDecompiler</a>
              trains multi-turn refinement using compiler and execution
              feedback. Their studies focus on functional recovery. For this
              work, I would use them as a starting point for the related task of
              finding source that matches a historical compiler's output.
            </p>
            <p>
              To reach such a well-defined task, though, we may first need to
              establish what a function owns or devise a new runtime
              measurement. In TH08, for example, a
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/commit/af9cb1d3e839b15a78b47ac1701506083cc82847"
                >collision difference led back to how the original program
                handled floating-point calculations</a
              >. Before the agent could test a useful correction, it had to work
              out what to measure.
            </p>
            <p>
              This suggests another role for a broadly capable model: helping
              make the problem checkable in the first place. Once that check
              exists, we can investigate how far a learned search policy can get
              with it.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="oracle">
            <h2>When the oracle is wrong</h2>
            <p>
              Once we train against that check, its coverage becomes part of
              what the model learns. I call the reference or checker an oracle.
              Even if it gives a repeatable answer, we still need to understand
              what that answer covers.
            </p>
            <p>
              TH08 showed how much can hide outside that coverage. Some
              reconstructed functions passed their exact comparisons with wrong
              floating constants. The comparator adjusted relocated addresses
              before checking instructions, but it checked the values behind
              those addresses only when they had been explicitly recorded for
              verification. Other literal references could pass without their
              values being examined.
            </p>
            <p>
              To close that gap, a
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/commit/a393f400786e4cc6cbb1000af0dad3276da12c78"
                >wider audit</a
              >
              found incorrect constants in functions that had already been
              accepted. In one calculation, the reconstructed source added
              <code>1.0f</code> where the original added <code>32.0f</code>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >A learner rewarded by the old check could learn to produce the
                wrong reconstruction.</strong
              >
              We had not trained an RL model against this oracle; the observed
              failure was in the check itself. But using that check as a reward
              would have made the same blind spot part of the learning
              environment.
            </p>
            <p>
              A functional test can have a similar blind spot.
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.05370v1"
                >Differential-fuzzing research on LLM decompilers</a
              >
              has found behavior differences beyond the supplied tests. Passing
              those tests therefore needs to be read in terms of the cases they
              exercise, just as an instruction match needs to be read in terms
              of the bytes and data it checks.
            </p>
            <p>
              Before using either kind of check as a reward, I would test it
              with deliberate failures. I would also keep final acceptance
              outside the candidate's editable source, so the model has to
              satisfy the check through its reconstruction. Otherwise, something
              like a byte array copied from the target could satisfy a naive
              score without recovering useful source.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >Improving the learner and improving its oracle belong to the
                same research problem.</strong
              >
              As the learner gets better at optimizing the feedback we give it,
              understanding that feedback becomes more important too.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="comparison">
            <h2>A comparison worth running</h2>
            <p>
              With those checks in place, I would start the comparison on a
              fixed VC7.1 task family. First, I would establish baselines for a
              strong general model and a smaller one. Adding repository
              knowledge to the smaller model would show what retrieval changes.
              From there, supervised specialization and an RL-trained version
              would let us examine what training adds.
            </p>
            <p>
              To make those comparisons informative, each model would receive
              the same target evidence and tools. For the training-specific
              comparisons, I would keep the base model and available knowledge
              fixed as well. Repeated trials would help distinguish a reliable
              search strategy from a lucky candidate.
            </p>
            <p>
              I would then separate two starting conditions: reconstructing a
              function from its instructions, and closing the last few
              differences in maintained source. A specialist might be excellent
              at the second task while a general model does much of the work
              needed to reach it. Measuring them separately would reveal that
              division of work.
            </p>
            <p>
              Both tasks need genuinely held-out examples. Because related
              Touhou games share engine code, holding out a game's name would
              still allow nearly identical functions to appear in training. I
              would instead hold out source families and keep their accepted
              answers out of both training and retrieval.
            </p>
            <p>
              On those examples, the outcome would be complete, independently
              validated matches. Giving each model the same candidate or compile
              budget would help compare search quality. Measuring elapsed time
              and total cost, including compilation, would tell us whether that
              quality produces an efficient workflow. Training costs would need
              their own accounting too.
            </p>
            <p>
              Both views matter because a cheaper proposal can become an
              expensive workflow if it causes more uninformative compiles.
              Conversely, a slower model may pay for itself by finding a useful
              experiment sooner. The comparison should capture those costs.
            </p>
            <p>
              Counting accepted results also keeps intermediate progress in
              perspective. One TH09
              <a
                href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th09/commit/ab72fd77728293fc0beacfc656ca701b0e266016"
                >replay-save candidate</a
              >
              looked promising because the compiled function had the expected
              size, but a full comparison still found substantial differences.
              Changes in how the compiler arranges instructions can also make a
              candidate look closer without resolving the underlying problem.
              Such diagnostics can guide the next experiment, but I would
              validate any reward for "getting closer" against the full
              acceptance check.
            </p>
            <p>
              I would be interested in the pattern across these results. If a
              specialist does well within a familiar compiler family, does that
              advantage survive a toolchain change? And once both models have
              good retrieval, how much does training still add? The answers
              would help us decide where each kind of model is most useful.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="future-agent">
            <h2>What kind of agent does this suggest?</h2>
            <p>
              If the models turn out to help at different stages, a useful
              arrangement might be a broad agent with access to a specialist.
              Once a task becomes a familiar compiler search, it could ask the
              specialist for candidates. If the results stop making sense, the
              broader agent could return to the target evidence or investigate
              the measurement itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              In that arrangement, specialization becomes a tool the agent can
              choose as the investigation develops. The agent keeps the freedom
              to change direction that made the earlier breakthroughs possible.
            </p>
            <p>
              This follows naturally from the industrial shift I described in
              the previous essay. Being able to run many more experiments
              changes how we organize the work. It also gives us a reason to ask
              which decisions need broad judgment and which improve through
              repeated experience with a particular craft.
            </p>
            <p>
              For now, the strongest general model has earned its place in my
              workflow by opening problems I had left unresolved. Those
              successes are what make me want to examine the work more closely:
              how much depended on the model's breadth, and how much could be
              handled by a model trained in this particular kind of
              reconstruction?
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong
                >The repositories give us a way to test the question.</strong
              >
              I would like to use that record to build the comparison and see
              what we learn.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section class="article-section" id="sources">
            <details class="native-details">
              <summary>Evidence and model attribution</summary>
              <p>
                <a
                  href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/website/evidence/reverse-engineering-models.md"
                  >Detailed evidence notes</a
