                    ><input
                      type="range"
                      min="2"
                      max="18"
                      step="0.1"
                      value="6"
                      data-dino-slider
                  /></label>
                  <label class="dino-auto-label"
                    ><input type="checkbox" data-dino-auto checked /> Use the
                    recovered acceleration rule</label
                  >
                </div>
                <p class="dino-status" data-dino-status role="status">
                  Original rule: start at 6, then increase toward 13.
                </p>
                <figcaption>
                  Move the slider to set your own speed. Space or Jump clears a
                  cactus.
                </figcaption>
                <noscript
                  ><p class="caption">
                    Enable JavaScript to play. You can still read the rule and
                    open the source code below.
                  </p></noscript
                >
              </figure>
              <a class="text-link" href="examples/dino-lab/"
                >Open the speed lab →</a
              >
            </div>
          </div>
          <details class="home-evidence-details">
            <summary>
              See the script, checks and how to try the analysis
            </summary>
            <p class="caption">
              Inspected target:
              <a href="https://wayou.github.io/t-rex-runner/"
                >wayou’s Chromium-derived browser edition</a
              >.
            </p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <div class="code-header">
                <span
                  >REA · loaded <span translate="no">index.js</span> · selected
                  lines</span
                >
              </div>
              <pre><code>if (this.currentSpeed &lt; this.config.MAX_SPEED) {
  <span class="code-key">this.currentSpeed += this.config.ACCELERATION;</span>
}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="dino-rule">
              Start at 6. Add 0.001 each update without a collision, while speed
              is below 13.
            </p>
            <figure class="workflow-figure dino-loop">
              <p class="figure-title">What each part does</p>
              <ol class="flow-steps">
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label">01 · REA</span
                  ><span class="flow-node">Read the running game’s script</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >Return its actual code and settings to the agent.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label">02 · Your agent</span
                  ><span class="flow-node">Rebuild the rule, add a control</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >Use the recovered acceleration in a small game with a speed
                    slider.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label">03 · You</span
                  ><span class="flow-node">Change the speed and play</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >Try the original acceleration, then choose your own
                    pace.</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ol>
            </figure>
            <p>
              REA inspected the HTTP browser edition through a local debugging
              connection and returned the loaded <code>index.js</code>,
              including its source and digest.
            </p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <div class="code-header">
                <span>Selected settings from the inspected script</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>ACCELERATION: 0.001,
MAX_SPEED: 13,
SPEED: 6</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p>
              We called the original game’s update function in a controlled
              browser check, with obstacles and automatic scheduling disabled.
              After 4,000 updates, speed was 10.0; after 10,000, it was 13.0,
              rounded to one decimal.
            </p>
            <p>
              The new mini-game keeps that speed rule. Its drawing, jumping and
              collision code are a small teaching implementation.
              <a href="examples/dino-lab/">Open the lab</a> to see the new code
              and run the same speed check.
            </p>
            <p>
              To inspect the target yourself, follow the
              <a href="guides/browser/#prepare">browser connection steps</a>
              using
              <a href="https://wayou.github.io/t-rex-runner/"
                >the dinosaur page</a
              >. Give your agent that page URL and your local debugging
              endpoint, then copy the prompt above.
            </p>
            <p class="caption">
              <a
                href="https://github.com/wayou/t-rex-runner/blob/5455bfa408ec6b707c7300ff194b7390733a766d/index.js#L565-L571"
                >Inspected speed code</a
              >
              ·
              <a href="assets/licenses/dinosaur.txt"
                >Chromium Authors · BSD license</a
              >
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="calculator">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <p class="eyebrow">Example 02 · Windows Calculator</p>
            <h2>Rebuild Calculator’s % button.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="lede">
            The % button does different things after + and ×. Let’s find both
            rules and try them in a small calculator.
          </p>
          <div class="home-example-grid">
            <div class="home-case-story">
              <h3>1. Ask about the % button</h3>
              <div class="agent-terminal">
                <div class="terminal-header">
                  <span class="terminal-title"
                    ><span class="terminal-dots" aria-hidden="true"
                      ><i></i><i></i><i></i></span
                    >Example question · Calculator</span
                  >
                  <button
                    class="copy-button"
                    type="button"
                    data-copy="calculator-analysis-prompt"
                    aria-label="Copy Calculator analysis prompt"
                  >
                    Copy
                  </button>
                </div>
                <p class="terminal-prompt">
                  <span class="prompt-symbol" aria-hidden="true">›</span
                  ><span id="calculator-analysis-prompt"
                    >Use REA to inspect Windows Calculator’s % button. Recover
                    the rules after + and ×, then build a small calculator that
                    uses both.</span
                  ><span class="terminal-cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </p>
              </div>
              <h3>2. Find both rules</h3>
              <p>
                REA helps the agent read the code behind the % button. It
                applies two different rules:
              </p>
              <dl class="percent-rules">
                <div>
                  <dt>With +</dt>
                  <dd>
                    <span>Take 10% of the first number.</span
                    ><code>10% of 200 = 20 → 200 + 20 = 220</code>
                  </dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <dt>With ×</dt>
                  <dd>
                    <span>Turn 10% into 0.1.</span><code>200 × 0.1 = 20</code>
                  </dd>
                </div>
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div class="home-case-result">
              <h3>3. Try the % button</h3>
              <p>
                We recreated both rules below. Choose + or ×, press %, then
                press =.
              </p>
              <figure class="percent-figure" data-percent-demo>
                <div
                  class="percent-operation"
                  role="group"
                  aria-label="Choose the calculation"
                  hidden
                >
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-percent-operation="add"
                    aria-pressed="true"
                  >
                    <span translate="no">200 + 10%</span>
                  </button>
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-percent-operation="multiply"
                    aria-pressed="false"
                  >
                    <span translate="no">200 × 10%</span>
                  </button>
                </div>
                <div class="percent-display">
                  <span class="percent-expression" data-percent-expression
                    >200 +</span
                  >
                  <strong data-percent-display>20</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="percent-controls" hidden>
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="percent-key"
                    data-percent-key="percent"
                  >
                    %
                  </button>
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="percent-key"
                    data-percent-key="equals"
                    disabled
                  >
                    =
                  </button>
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="percent-reset"
                    data-percent-key="reset"
                  >
                    Reset
                  </button>
                </div>
                <p class="percent-status" data-percent-status role="status">
                  10% of 200 = 20. Then 200 + 20 = 220.
                </p>
                <figcaption>
                  Interactive illustration of the inspected percentage rule.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </div>
          <details class="home-evidence-details">
            <summary>See the real code and how the answer was checked</summary>
            <p>
              For this example, <code>previous = 200</code> and
              <code>current = 10</code>.
            </p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <div class="code-header">
                <span>Readable summary of the % handler</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>if (operation == multiply || operation == divide) {
  percent = current / 100;
} else {
  <span class="code-key">percent = current * previous / 100;</span>
}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <figure class="workflow-figure calculator-trace">
              <p class="figure-title">
                From the installed app to a working % button
              </p>
              <ol class="flow-steps">
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label"
                    >01 · REA reads <span translate="no">calc.exe</span></span
                  ><span class="flow-node">It launches the Calculator app</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >The code opens <code>ms-calculator:</code>. The agent then
                    selects the installed app’s library.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label">02 · REA reads the library</span
                  ><span class="flow-node">Return the % handler’s code</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >The function checks the operator and uses the constant
                    <code>100</code>.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="flow-label">03 · Your agent interprets it</span
                  ><span class="flow-node">Explain and reproduce the rules</span
                  ><span class="flow-note"
                    >Crosscheck the branch with Microsoft’s source, then try +
                    and × above.</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ol>
            </figure>
            <div class="code-comparison">
              <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-header">
                  <span>REA · selected original instructions</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>0x180124945: MOV EAX, dword ptr [R13 + 0x18]
0x180124949: <span class="code-key">CMP EAX, 0x5c</span>
0x18012494c: JZ 0x180124aba
0x180124952: <span class="code-key">CMP EAX, 0x5b</span>
0x180124955: JZ 0x180124aba
0x18012495b: <span class="code-key">MOV EDX, 0x64</span></code></pre>
              </div>
              <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-header">
                  <span>Operator IDs · Microsoft’s source</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>#define IDC_MUL 92      // 0x5c
#define IDC_DIV 91      // 0x5b
#define IDC_PERCENT 118 // 0x76
0x64 = 100</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="caption">
              Inspected with REA 4.1.0: Windows Calculator 11.2508.4.0, x64. The
              readable summary uses names from Microsoft’s public source to
              explain the recovered branch.
            </p>
            <p class="caption">
              <a
                href="https://github.com/microsoft/calculator/blob/d125246a4e19842ce1332e6c7839cf0e110027d8/src/CalcManager/CEngine/scifunc.cpp#L98-L111"
                >Percentage implementation</a
              >
              ·
              <a
                href="https://github.com/microsoft/calculator/blob/d125246a4e19842ce1332e6c7839cf0e110027d8/src/CalculatorUnitTests/CalculatorManagerTest.cpp#L350-L370"
                >Microsoft’s test cases</a
              >
              · <a href="guides/native/">Native setup guide</a>
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="showcases">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="home-showcase-heading">
            <div>
              <h2>REA case studies.</h2>
              <p class="lede">
                Explore a feature in a released app or game, with code and
                checked results.
              </p>
            </div>
            <a class="text-link" href="showcase/">Showcases →</a>
          </div>
          <div class="home-case-grid">
            <article class="home-case-card">
              <p class="eyebrow">Android · APK analysis</p>
              <h3>How an authenticator generates a login code</h3>
              <p>
                Trace Aegis's six-digit login code through its released APK,
                then move a demo clock to see why it changes every 30 seconds.
              </p>
              <a class="text-link" href="showcase/aegis/"
                >Read the Android case study</a
              >
            </article>
            <article class="home-case-card">
              <p class="eyebrow">Electron · Feature tracing</p>
              <h3>How Notion copies text and HTML</h3>
              <p>
                Follow Notion's packaged JavaScript to the system clipboard and
                see how a copy preserves rich block data.
              </p>
              <a class="text-link" href="showcase/notion/"
                >Read the Notion case study</a
              >
            </article>
            <article class="home-case-card">
              <p class="eyebrow">Native · Reconstruction</p>
              <h3>DX-Ball's sound-pan calculation</h3>
              <p>
                Recover how a ball's position becomes left/right sound in a 1996
                Windows game.
              </p>
              <a class="text-link" href="showcase/dx-ball/"
                >Read the DX-Ball case study</a
              >
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="try-rea">
        <div class="container start-row">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Your first investigation</p>
            <h2>Try REA on a small app.</h2>
            <p>
              Download our Notes example, trace its CSV export, and check one
              changed input.
            </p>
          </div>
          <a class="button" href="first-investigation/"
            >Try the guided example</a
          >
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="analyze">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <h2>Choose your target.</h2>
            <p>
              These guides cover setup and a first investigation for each kind
              of software.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="workflows" id="analysis-guides">
            <div>
              <h3>Native binaries</h3>
              <p>
                Inspect functions, strings, references and call relationships in
                executables and libraries.
              </p>
              <a href="guides/native/">Native analysis guide</a>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>JavaScript &amp; Electron</h3>
              <p>
                Map modules, routes, IPC and native dependencies from an
                application folder or ASAR archive.
              </p>
              <a href="guides/javascript/">Application workflows</a>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Browser &amp; runtime activity</h3>
              <p>
                Capture selected browser or process activity, then compare the
                results across runs.
              </p>
              <a href="guides/browser/">Browser observation guide</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <details class="home-evidence-details" id="project-prompts">
            <summary>Try a larger project</summary>
            <div class="code-comparison home-project-prompts">
              <div>
                <div class="agent-terminal">
                  <div class="terminal-header">
                    <span class="terminal-title">
                      <span class="terminal-dots" aria-hidden="true"
                        ><i></i><i></i><i></i></span
                      >Example prompt · Website
                    </span>
                    <button
                      class="copy-button"
                      type="button"
                      data-copy="website-project-prompt"
                      aria-label="Copy website project prompt"
                    >
                      Copy
                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <p class="terminal-prompt">
                    <span class="prompt-symbol" aria-hidden="true">›</span
                    ><span id="website-project-prompt"
                      >Use REA to inspect
                      <span translate="no">https://rea.tools/</span>. Clone this
                      website for me.</span
                    ><span class="terminal-cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p class="caption">Yes, this one.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <div class="agent-terminal">
                  <div class="terminal-header">
                    <span class="terminal-title">
                      <span class="terminal-dots" aria-hidden="true"
                        ><i></i><i></i><i></i></span
                      >Example prompt · Game reconstruction
                    </span>
                    <button
                      class="copy-button"
                      type="button"
                      data-copy="game-project-prompt"
                      aria-label="Copy game reconstruction prompt"
                    >
                      Copy
                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <p class="terminal-prompt">
                    <span class="prompt-symbol" aria-hidden="true">›</span
                    ><span id="game-project-prompt"
                      >Use REA to reconstruct this game from its executable.
                      Recover the gameplay logic in C and test it against the
                      original.</span
                    ><span class="terminal-cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p class="caption">
                  <a href="showcase/dx-ball/">See the DX-Ball case study →</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="community">
        <div class="container">
          <div>
            <h2>Any questions?</h2>
            <div class="home-help-links">
              <p>
                <a href="faq/">Read the FAQ</a> — setup and common questions.
              </p>
              <p>
                <a href="https://discord.gg/GkcryMnJDM">Join Discord</a> — get
